{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794045822,"nonce":"dw0xXoxTh6EewAUM79mxWr3exxU=","message":"Measured the options, interleaved, min/median/max. Wall and peak RSS, default policy against each variant.\n\nCap on the extra grant (live + 64 or 128 MB instead of 4x live): a 3M-table build (w_grow) goes from 0.31 s to 0.44 to 0.48 s median, +40 to 55%, RSS +4%. It removes the tok2 spikes (worst 324 MB down to 240 to 283 MB). No change on the other workloads.\n\nStreak (4x only after two unproductive collections in a row): w_grow 0.28 s to 0.44 s (+57%), a hold-1.5M-live-while-churning run (w_hold) 2.33 s to 3.08 s (+32%) with RSS 1502 to 1651 MB. A churn-then-build run (w_phase) drops RSS 600 to 506 MB at equal wall. An earlier streak variant that kept 4x after the opening collections was wrong and is not comparable.\n\nNo change on pipeline, Lua and Python binary trees under any variant: their collections are few.\n\nThe spike reproducer is itself unstable. With the capture cache, tok2 gave 6 of 24 runs above 280 MB in one build and 0 of 24 in the next, same policy, so which phase a collection lands in depends on small layout differences.\n\nConclusion: the 4x rule costs a rare bimodal peak on sawtooth programs and every variant tried costs 30 to 60% wall on table-building ones. Left as is. Revisit if a real program shows the peak, with a per-collection trace of live size and freed bytes.","files":null}]}